<!-- PENSIONS, TAXES & SARS EMIGRATION -->
<section class="msv__section--dark">
  <div class="msv__container">
    <div class="msv__section-hd">
      <h2>Pensions, Taxes & SARS Emigration</h2>
      <p>South African tax residency and pension portability are critical topics for retirees. Understanding your obligations with SARS and proper tax planning can save thousands of ZAR.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="msv__two-col">
      <div class="msv__two-col-card">
        <span class="msv__two-col-icon">👤</span>
        <h3>Ceasing Tax Residency (SARS)</h3>
        <p>South Africa taxes its residents on worldwide income. To stop this, you must formally cease being a tax resident. This requires:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Leaving South Africa with intent not to return</li>
          <li>Notifying SARS in writing (Form IT 230)</li>
          <li>Providing evidence of relocation (lease, employment, residency permit)</li>
          <li>Breaking ties with South Africa (selling property, resigning from positions)</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0;"><strong>Important:</strong> You're not automatically non-resident upon leaving. You must formally apply and receive SARS confirmation. Failure to do so means continued South African tax liability.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="msv__two-col-card">
        <span class="msv__two-col-icon">💼</span>
        <h3>Pension Portability Options</h3>
        <p>South African retirement funds have specific rules about overseas withdrawal:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Retirement annuities can be transferred to foreign providers</li>
          <li>Pension funds often require withdrawal as lump sum upon emigration</li>
          <li>Lump sum withdrawals are taxable in South Africa (sliding scale: 0% to 45%)</li>
          <li>Carefully plan withdrawal timing to minimize tax impact</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0;"><strong>Tip:</strong> Some retirees spread withdrawals across years to stay in lower tax brackets. Consult a tax professional familiar with emigration rules.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--warn">
      <span class="msv__alert-icon">⚠️</span>
      <p><strong>No South Africa-Spain Tax Treaty:</strong> Unlike many countries, South Africa and Spain have no double-taxation agreement. This means you may be taxed on the same income by both countries. For example, if you have property rental income from South Africa and are now tax resident in Spain, you might owe tax in both jurisdictions. Work with a cross-border tax advisor to structure your income to minimize exposure.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="msv__two-col" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
      <div class="msv__two-col-card">
        <span class="msv__two-col-icon">📋</span>
        <h3>Spanish Tax Residency</h3>
        <p>Once you register in Spain (empadronamiento), you become tax resident. Spain taxes residents on worldwide income, but offers lower rates on certain retirement income. Spanish personal income tax rates range from 19% to 45%, with deductions and allowances for retirees.</p>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">Register at your local municipal office within 30 days of arrival.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="msv__two-col-card">
        <span class="msv__two-col-icon">🔗</span>
        <h3>PRSI & Social Security</h3>
        <p>If you receive a South African pension, Spain may require social security contributions. This varies based on your specific situation. Additionally, some retirees maintain voluntary SARS compliance to protect South African benefits (e.g., tax exemption thresholds).</p>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">Discuss this with both South African and Spanish tax advisors.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--info">
      <span class="msv__alert-icon">ℹ️</span>
      <p><strong>Professional Help Essential:</strong> Tax emigration and pension portability decisions are complex and have lifelong implications. We recommend consulting with a certified tax advisor who specializes in South African emigration and Spanish tax law. The cost of advice (typically R5,000-20,000) is easily offset by proper tax planning.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

