      <div class="msv__two-col-card">
        <span class="msv__two-col-icon">🏥</span>
        <h3>Register with Healthcare System</h3>
        <p>After registration at the town hall, you're eligible for public healthcare. Visit your local health center (centro de salud) to register and request a family doctor (médico de cabecera). This is free and essential for all healthcare needs.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="msv__two-col-card">
        <span class="msv__two-col-icon">🚗</span>
        <h3>Convert Your South African Driving License</h3>
        <p>Your South African license is valid for visits, but to drive as a resident, obtain a Spanish driving license. You can exchange it without retesting at the DGT (Dirección General de Tráfico). Bring your South African license, passport, and NIE.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--info">
      <span class="msv__alert-icon">ℹ️</span>
      <p><strong>Language Support:</strong> Most government services offer English assistance, especially in major cities. However, learning basic Spanish (even A1 level) will significantly ease your settling-in process and improve your social integration. Consider enrolling in a language course once you arrive.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- COMMON MISTAKES -->
<section class="msv__section--dark">
  <div class="msv__container">
    <div class="msv__section-hd">
      <h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
      <p>Learn from the experience of hundreds of South African retirees who've made the transition. Here are pitfalls to sidestep.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--red">
      <span class="msv__alert-icon">❌</span>
      <p><strong>Mistake 1: Ignoring SARB Exchange Controls:</strong> Many South Africans attempt to transfer large sums without complying with SARB regulations. This can result in asset freezes and penalties. Always notify SARB and apply for non-resident status if transferring over R11 million. Use licensed currency brokers and keep records.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--red">
      <span class="msv__alert-icon">❌</span>
      <p><strong>Mistake 2: Forgetting SARS Tax Emigration:</strong> Assuming you're automatically non-resident in South Africa once you leave is a critical error. You must formally apply to SARS using Form IT 230. Failure to do so means continued worldwide tax liability, even after moving to Spain.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--red">
      <span class="msv__alert-icon">❌</span>
      <p><strong>Mistake 3: Not Apostilling Documents Properly:</strong> DIRCO apostilles are non-negotiable. Don't assume regular notarization is sufficient. All South African public documents (police clearance, birth certificate, marriage certificates) must be apostilled through DIRCO. Without proper apostilles, your NLV application will be rejected.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--red">
      <span class="msv__alert-icon">❌</span>
      <p><strong>Mistake 4: Overlooking the Double Taxation Issue:</strong> The lack of a South Africa-Spain tax treaty means careful tax planning is essential. Don't assume you'll automatically avoid taxation in one jurisdiction. Consult with cross-border tax specialists before making pension withdrawal decisions or establishing income structures.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--red">
      <span class="msv__alert-icon">❌</span>
      <p><strong>Mistake 5: Missing NLV Documentation Deadlines:</strong> The 90-day window to enter Spain after visa approval is firm. Also, empadronamiento and TIE registration must happen within specific timeframes. Missing deadlines can result in visa cancellation. Use a calendar and set reminders.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--red">
      <span class="msv__alert-icon">❌</span>
      <p><strong>Mistake 6: Underestimating Language Barriers:</strong> While many Spaniards speak English in tourism areas, rural areas and government offices often don't. Learning basic Spanish before arrival will ease healthcare access, banking, and social integration. Consider online courses before leaving South Africa.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- FAQ SECTION -->
<section class="msv__section">
  <div class="msv__container">
    <div class="msv__section-hd">
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <p>Answers to common questions from South African retirees planning their move to Spain.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="msv__faq-list">
      <details>
        <summary>What is the Non-Lucrative Visa (NLV) for South Africans?</summary>
        <div class="msv__faq-answer">
          <p>The Non-Lucrative Visa is a Spanish residence permit designed for retirees and individuals with passive income who don't work in Spain. South Africans can apply if they meet the financial requirements (approximately €2,400 monthly or €850,000+ lump sum) and pass police clearance. It's one of the most accessible routes for South African retirees seeking permanent Spanish residency.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>How much money do I need to retire to Spain from South Africa?</summary>
        <div class="msv__faq-answer">
          <p>The Spanish government requires approximately €2,400 per month in passive income (roughly ZAR 48,000/month) or a lump sum of €850,000+ (approximately ZAR 17,000,000+). These requirements vary by autonomous community. South Africans should budget for exchange control regulations and SARB approval when transferring funds.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Do I need a visa as a South African to retire in Spain?</summary>
        <div class="msv__faq-answer">
          <p>Yes. South African passport holders are not EU citizens and require a long-term residence visa. The Non-Lucrative Visa is the primary option for retirees. Additionally, South Africans need a Schengen visa for any visits to Spain before obtaining the NLV. Initial entry is typically on a tourist visa.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>What are the SARS tax obligations when retiring to Spain?</summary>
        <div class="msv__faq-answer">
          <p>South African retirees must notify SARS about their intention to cease being a tax resident. This involves submitting a tax emigration application. It's crucial to understand South Africa's worldwide income tax rules and the fact that there is no South Africa-Spain tax treaty, which may result in double taxation on certain income types. Professional tax advice is essential.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>What police clearance do South Africans need for Spain?</summary>
        <div class="msv__faq-answer">
          <p>South Africans must obtain a police clearance certificate (SAPS clearance) from the South African Police Service. This certificate must be apostilled through DIRCO (Department of International Relations and Cooperation). The clearance must be issued within the last three months and is a mandatory requirement for the Non-Lucrative Visa application.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>How do I transfer money from South Africa to Spain?</summary>
        <div class="msv__faq-answer">
          <p>South African residents must comply with SARB (South African Reserve Bank) exchange control regulations. Non-residents can transfer funds more freely, but residents face limits (typically R11 million per calendar year for individuals). It's advisable to apply for non-resident status with SARB before making large transfers and to use licensed currency brokers for better rates than banks.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Are South African pensions portable to Spain?</summary>
        <div class="msv__faq-answer">
          <p>South African retirement annuities and pension funds can often be transferred abroad or withdrawn as a lump sum, subject to tax implications and regulatory approval. However, some funds may impose restrictions. Professional financial advice is essential to understand withdrawal options, tax consequences in South Africa, and how your pension interacts with Spanish tax residence status.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>What is the cost of living comparison between South Africa and Spain?</summary>
        <div class="msv__faq-answer">
          <p>Generally, Spain is more affordable than major South African cities for retirees. Rent in Spanish cities (€400-800/month for a one-bedroom apartment outside city centers) is comparable to Cape Town or Johannesburg, but groceries and healthcare are often cheaper. Healthcare through Spain's public system is significantly less expensive than private healthcare in South Africa.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>How long does the Non-Lucrative Visa approval process take?</summary>
        <div class="msv__faq-answer">
          <p>The process typically takes 2-4 months from initial application to approval, though timeframes vary by consulate. After approval, you must enter Spain and process the TIE (Residency Card) at the National Police headquarters, which can take several weeks. Total timeline from start to having your TIE is usually 3-6 months.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Can I apply for Spanish citizenship as a South African retiree?</summary>
        <div class="msv__faq-answer">
          <p>Yes, after residing in Spain legally for 10 years on the Non-Lucrative Visa or other permits, you can apply for Spanish citizenship. This requires passing Spanish language proficiency and cultural knowledge tests, renouncing your South African citizenship (unless Spain permits dual citizenship in your case), and meeting other residency and character requirements. Some South Africans qualify for citizenship pathways through ancestry as well.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

